-- Q1: medium / L2 — Hash totals in batch processing
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Greenfield Corp processes payroll in weekly batches. Before submitting a batch of 200 payroll transactions, the payroll supervisor calculates the sum of all employee Social Security numbers in the batch. This sum is compared to the same calculation after processing. Which type of batch control total does this represent?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Record count", "Hash total", "Financial total", "Run-to-run total"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS06.02, a hash total sums a non-financial field (such as Social Security numbers) to verify batch completeness. The sum has no financial meaning but detects missing or added records during processing. Wrong (A): A record count tracks only the number of transactions, not the content of specific fields. Wrong (C): A financial total sums monetary amounts such as gross pay, not non-financial identifiers. Wrong (D): A run-to-run total reconciles ending totals of one processing cycle to the beginning of the next, not within a single batch.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Timing differences at integration points
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Hillcrest Retail imports daily sales data from its e-commerce platform into its ERP general ledger via a nightly batch feed. The e-commerce platform records sales in real time, but the GL reflects them only after the overnight import. Which risk does this timing difference most directly introduce?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Unauthorized access to the API", "Temporary misstatement of financial balances between systems", "Corruption of master data records", "Loss of source documents"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS06.04, when systems process at different intervals, temporary mismatches arise between the source and target systems. Until the nightly import runs, the GL understates revenue and receivables relative to the e-commerce platform. Wrong (A): Unauthorized API access is an authentication risk, not a timing risk. Wrong (C): Master data corruption relates to mapping errors during transformation, not processing frequency differences. Wrong (D): Source documents are retained in the originating platform regardless of import timing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Master data fraud via vendor bank account change
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$An internal auditor at Thornton Industries discovers that a purchasing clerk changed a vendor's bank account number in the ERP master file two days before a $340,000 payment was issued. The new bank account belongs to a shell company. No secondary approval was required for the master data change. Which control failure is the ROOT cause of this fraud?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Lack of three-way matching on the purchase order", "Failure to encrypt vendor payment files in transit", "Absence of dual-approval controls over master data modifications", "Insufficient segregation of duties in the payment approval process"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT DSS06.03, master data changes are high-risk because they affect all subsequent transactions. A fraudulent change to a vendor bank account redirects every future payment. Dual-approval controls over master data modifications would have required independent verification before the change took effect. Wrong (A): Three-way matching validates invoice-to-PO-to-receipt agreement but does not prevent misdirected payments from a compromised master file. Wrong (B): Encryption protects data in transit but does not prevent unauthorized changes to master records. Wrong (D): Payment approval segregation is important but would not catch a payment routed to a legitimately-appearing vendor record that was fraudulently modified.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Data corruption from mapping errors at integration points
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Pineview Manufacturing integrates its standalone procurement system with a new ERP. During the first month, several purchase orders transferred from the procurement system are posted to incorrect GL accounts in the ERP. Which control would MOST effectively prevent this type of data corruption?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Encrypting all data transmitted between systems", "Implementing data validation rules and exception reporting on mapped fields", "Increasing the frequency of data transfers from daily to hourly", "Requiring manual re-entry of all purchase orders into the ERP"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS06.04, data validation rules and exception reporting at integration points detect mapping errors that transform data incorrectly during transfer. Exception reports flag transactions where account codes do not match expected values, allowing correction before posting. Wrong (A): Encryption protects data confidentiality in transit but does not detect field-mapping errors. Wrong (C): Increasing transfer frequency does not address the root cause of incorrect account mapping. Wrong (D): Manual re-entry eliminates automation benefits and introduces additional human error risk.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — ERP single point of failure and business continuity
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Larkspur Financial converted all departments to a single ERP system last quarter. During a system outage lasting six hours, no department could process transactions, generate reports, or access customer data. Which ERP-specific risk does this scenario illustrate, and what is the MOST appropriate mitigating control?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Excessive customization risk; mitigated by limiting system modifications", "Single point of failure risk; mitigated by a documented business continuity plan with failover capability", "Data migration risk; mitigated by parallel running of old and new systems", "Vendor lock-in risk; mitigated by adopting open-source ERP software"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS04.03, an integrated ERP represents a single point of failure — when it goes down, all business functions are affected simultaneously. A business continuity plan with failover capability ensures critical operations can resume within acceptable timeframes. Wrong (A): Excessive customization introduces upgrade and maintenance risks, but does not describe the scenario of total operational shutdown during an outage. Wrong (C): Data migration risk applies during system conversion, not during ongoing operations. Wrong (D): Vendor lock-in concerns long-term flexibility, not immediate operational resilience during outages.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — AIS six fundamental components
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Which of the following is one of the six fundamental components of an accounting information system (AIS)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["External audit reports", "Internal controls", "Marketing analytics", "Competitive benchmarks"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA IT Section guidance, the six AIS components are people, procedures and instructions, data, software, IT infrastructure, and internal controls. Internal controls safeguard data, ensure accuracy, and enforce compliance within the system. Wrong (A): External audit reports are outputs produced by auditors, not a component of the AIS itself. Wrong (C): Marketing analytics support business strategy but are not a fundamental AIS component. Wrong (D): Competitive benchmarks are management tools unrelated to the core structure of an AIS.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Reconciliation controls between source and target systems
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Ridgeline Services transmits 450 invoice records nightly from its billing platform to the ERP accounts receivable module. After this morning's import, the AR module shows only 447 invoices. Which reconciliation control should have detected this discrepancy BEFORE the data was posted?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Comparing record counts and dollar totals between the source system export and target system import", "Requiring manual approval of each invoice before import", "Encrypting the data file during transmission", "Running a duplicate invoice check on the imported records"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT DSS06.04, reconciliation of record counts and totals between source and target systems is the primary control for detecting data loss at integration points. Comparing the 450-record export to the 447-record import would immediately reveal three missing transactions. Wrong (B): Manual approval of each invoice is impractical at volume and does not specifically detect missing records during transfer. Wrong (C): Encryption protects confidentiality but does not detect whether all records arrived. Wrong (D): Duplicate checks identify repeated records but do not detect records lost in transit.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Audit trail immutability and write-once storage
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Cedarbrook Holdings requires that its ERP audit log entries cannot be modified or deleted by any user, including system administrators. The IT director is evaluating approaches to enforce this requirement. Which approach provides the STRONGEST assurance of audit trail integrity?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restricting audit log access to the IT security team", "Storing audit logs on a separate write-once storage server", "Backing up audit logs daily to an offsite location", "Requiring dual authorization for any changes to log entries"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS05.07, write-once storage (WORM) prevents any user — including administrators — from modifying or deleting log entries after they are written. This provides the strongest technical enforcement of immutability. Wrong (A): Restricting access reduces the risk but still allows authorized users to alter logs. Wrong (C): Daily backups preserve a copy but do not prevent modification of the primary log between backups. Wrong (D): Dual authorization adds a procedural control but still permits changes if two parties collude.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — Edit checks flagging unusual transactions
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Maple Creek Corp's ERP system flags a vendor invoice for $875,000 because the amount exceeds the configured threshold of $500,000 for that vendor category. This automated alert is an example of which type of control?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Edit check", "Batch control total", "Output control", "Run-to-run total"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT DSS06.02, an edit check identifies unusual or potentially erroneous transactions by comparing input values against predefined thresholds or parameters. Flagging an invoice exceeding a dollar threshold is a classic edit check. Wrong (B): A batch control total reconciles aggregate amounts before and after batch processing, not individual transaction amounts. Wrong (C): An output control governs report distribution and access to processed results, not data entry validation. Wrong (D): A run-to-run total compares ending balances of one processing cycle to the beginning of the next.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — API authentication at integration points
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Northgate Distribution connects its e-commerce storefront to the ERP order management module through a RESTful API. The IT auditor is concerned that an unauthorized third party could submit fraudulent sales orders through the API endpoint. Which control MOST directly addresses this risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Implementing three-way matching on all purchase orders", "Requiring API authentication with OAuth tokens and access logging", "Increasing the batch processing frequency to detect anomalies faster", "Encrypting the ERP database at rest"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS05.04, API authentication ensures that only authorized systems can submit data through integration endpoints. OAuth tokens verify the caller's identity, and access logging creates a trail of all API interactions for monitoring. Wrong (A): Three-way matching applies to the expenditure cycle and does not prevent unauthorized order submission. Wrong (C): Increasing processing frequency does not authenticate the source of incoming data. Wrong (D): Database encryption at rest protects stored data but does not prevent unauthorized submissions through the API.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Cost flow methods in inventory module
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Brookstone Manufacturing configures its ERP inventory module to track cost layers so that the oldest purchased units are expensed first when inventory is sold. Which cost flow method has Brookstone selected in its ERP configuration?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Weighted average", "Last-in, first-out (LIFO)", "First-in, first-out (FIFO)", "Specific identification"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per ASC 330-10-30, FIFO assumes the oldest (first-in) inventory costs are expensed first when goods are sold. The ERP tracks cost layers chronologically and applies the earliest layers to cost of goods sold. Wrong (A): Weighted average blends all purchase costs into a single average rather than maintaining separate cost layers. Wrong (B): LIFO expenses the most recent (last-in) costs first, the opposite of what is described. Wrong (D): Specific identification tracks actual costs of individually identifiable items, not chronological layers.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Transaction data definition
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Which of the following is an example of transaction data in an ERP system?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Chart of accounts", "Vendor master file", "Employee pay rate table", "Individual sales invoice"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT APO03.02, transaction data records individual business events that occur continuously. A sales invoice is created with each sale and represents a specific business event. Wrong (A): The chart of accounts is master data — a relatively static reference structure that defines account categories. Wrong (B): The vendor master file is master data containing reference information about suppliers. Wrong (C): Employee pay rate tables are master data that change infrequently and require authorization to modify.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Change management controls for master data
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Waverly Corp's internal audit team discovers that 14 changes to customer credit limits in the ERP master file were made over the past quarter without any documented approval. The changes were all made by a single AR clerk. Which control improvement would MOST effectively address this finding?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Implementing an automated workflow requiring manager approval before any master data change takes effect", "Adding encryption to the customer master file", "Increasing the frequency of customer credit reviews from quarterly to monthly", "Requiring the AR clerk to document changes in a spreadsheet after the fact"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT DSS06.03, master data changes are high-risk because they affect all subsequent transactions. An automated approval workflow prevents unauthorized modifications by requiring independent authorization before changes are saved, creating an enforceable control rather than relying on manual compliance. Wrong (B): Encryption protects data confidentiality but does not enforce authorization requirements for changes. Wrong (C): More frequent credit reviews address business decisions but do not control who can modify the master file. Wrong (D): After-the-fact documentation is a detective control that does not prevent unauthorized changes.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Real-time reporting benefit of integrated ERP
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Clearwater Logistics recently replaced five standalone departmental applications with a single ERP system sharing one database. The CFO reports that month-end close now takes three days instead of twelve. Which ERP benefit MOST directly explains this improvement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Lower software licensing costs from consolidating vendors", "Real-time reporting from integrated data eliminates manual reconciliation between separate systems", "Reduced need for IT staff to maintain multiple platforms", "Improved vendor negotiating power through consolidated purchasing data"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT APO03.02, a single integrated database eliminates data inconsistencies across departments. When all modules share one database, there is no need to manually reconcile figures between separate systems, which is the primary driver of month-end close delays. Wrong (A): Licensing cost reduction is a financial benefit but does not explain faster month-end close. Wrong (C): Reduced IT staffing needs improve operational efficiency but do not directly accelerate financial reporting. Wrong (D): Consolidated purchasing data supports procurement but is unrelated to the speed of financial close.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Four elements captured by an audit trail
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$What four elements does a complete audit trail in an ERP system capture for each transaction or event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Cost, revenue, profit, and margin", "Who, what, when, and where", "Input, processing, output, and storage", "Plan, do, check, and act"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS05.07, a complete audit trail records who initiated or approved the transaction (user ID), what was changed (before and after values), when the change occurred (timestamp), and where in the system it was made (module, screen, field). Wrong (A): Cost, revenue, profit, and margin are financial metrics, not audit trail elements. Wrong (C): Input, processing, output, and storage describe AIS data flow stages, not audit trail content. Wrong (D): Plan, do, check, and act is the Deming cycle for quality management, not an audit trail framework.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Bill of Materials in manufacturing module
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Ashland Manufacturing uses its ERP production module to plan work orders for a product that requires three raw materials in specified quantities. The system references a structured list of all components and their quantities needed to produce one finished unit. Which ERP data element is the system referencing?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Routing table", "Chart of accounts", "Bill of Materials (BOM)", "Vendor master file"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT BAI03.03, a Bill of Materials (BOM) is a structured list of all raw materials, components, and subassemblies required to produce one unit of a finished product, including quantities. The manufacturing module uses the BOM to plan material requirements. Wrong (A): A routing table defines the sequence of production steps and work centers, not the list of required components. Wrong (B): The chart of accounts is a GL reference structure for categorizing financial transactions. Wrong (D): The vendor master file contains supplier information, not product composition details.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Backup and data retention as storage controls
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Ferndale Corp's AIS administrator schedules nightly encrypted backups of the ERP database to an offsite location with a seven-year retention policy. This practice is an example of which type of AIS control?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Storage control", "Input control", "Processing control", "Output control"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT DSS04.07, storage controls protect data integrity through backup, encryption, and retention policies. Nightly encrypted backups to an offsite location ensure data can be recovered after loss, and the retention policy ensures compliance with record-keeping requirements. Wrong (B): Input controls validate and authorize data at the point of entry into the system. Wrong (C): Processing controls ensure calculations and posting logic execute correctly during transaction processing. Wrong (D): Output controls govern the distribution of and access to reports and other system outputs.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — Cutoff risk from integration timing differences
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Stratford Group's payroll provider transmits payroll journal entries to the ERP general ledger via a weekly batch file every Monday morning. The December 31 year-end falls on a Wednesday, but the final payroll batch covering December 29-31 is not transmitted until the following Monday, January 5. The external auditor identifies a $215,000 payroll accrual that is missing from the December 31 trial balance. Which integration-related control would have MOST effectively prevented this cutoff error?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Encrypting payroll data files during transmission", "Implementing scheduled reconciliation and cutoff procedures that trigger an accrual entry when batch timing crosses a reporting period boundary", "Requiring dual approval on all payroll journal entries", "Switching from batch processing to real-time processing for all payroll transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS06.04, timing differences between systems create mismatches that require scheduled reconciliation and cutoff procedures. A cutoff procedure would detect that the December 29-31 payroll had not yet been imported and trigger an accrual entry to ensure completeness of the period-end financial statements. Wrong (A): Encryption protects data confidentiality but does not address period-end cutoff timing. Wrong (C): Dual approval controls authorization of entries but does not detect missing entries caused by batch timing. Wrong (D): Real-time processing may reduce timing gaps but is impractical for payroll, which is inherently batch-oriented due to pay period cycles.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Standardized business processes as ERP benefit
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Which of the following is a primary benefit of implementing an ERP system across an organization?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Elimination of all manual business processes", "Reduced need for internal controls", "Standardized business processes that reduce manual intervention", "Guaranteed prevention of data entry errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT APO03.02, ERP systems enforce standardized business processes across departments, reducing inconsistencies and manual workarounds. Standardization streamlines operations and improves efficiency. Wrong (A): ERP systems automate many processes but do not eliminate all manual activities — human judgment and oversight remain necessary. Wrong (B): ERP systems actually require robust internal controls due to the concentration of data in a single system. Wrong (D): No system can guarantee prevention of all data entry errors; ERP systems reduce errors through validation but cannot eliminate them entirely.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Before-and-after values in audit trail for fraud detection
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$During a fraud investigation at Mercer Industries, forensic auditors need to determine who changed a supplier's payment terms from Net-30 to Net-0 (immediate payment) and what the original values were. The ERP system's audit log records only user IDs, timestamps, and the module accessed — but does not capture field-level before-and-after values. Which audit trail deficiency does this scenario expose, and what is the MOST significant consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Failure to capture the 'where' element; auditors cannot determine which system module was used", "Failure to capture the 'when' element; auditors cannot establish a timeline of events", "Failure to capture the 'who' element; auditors cannot identify the responsible user", "Failure to capture the 'what' element; auditors cannot determine the specific change made or reconstruct the original data"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT DSS05.07, a complete audit trail must capture what was changed, including before-and-after values. Without field-level change data, auditors cannot determine what specific modification was made, reconstruct the original payment terms, or establish evidence of intentional manipulation. Wrong (A): The scenario states the module accessed is logged, so the "where" element is captured. Wrong (B): Timestamps are recorded, so the "when" element is captured. Wrong (C): User IDs are recorded, so the "who" element is captured.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Source documents as AIS input
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Belmont Enterprises receives a vendor invoice for office supplies. An accounts payable clerk enters the invoice details into the AIS. In the AIS data flow, the vendor invoice serves as which element?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A source document captured as input", "A processing control", "An output report", "A master data record"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA IT Section guidance, source documents such as invoices, purchase orders, and receipts are the starting point of AIS data flow. They are captured as inputs through manual entry or automated import before processing begins. Wrong (B): Processing controls are automated checks applied during transaction processing, not the documents themselves. Wrong (C): Output reports are the results of processing, such as financial statements and dashboards. Wrong (D): Master data records are relatively static reference data like vendor names and addresses, not individual transaction documents.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L3 — Automated approval routing and threshold override risk
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Crestview Corp configures its ERP workflow engine so that purchase orders under $25,000 require only department manager approval, while orders at or above $25,000 require VP approval. An internal auditor discovers that several departments routinely split large purchases into multiple orders just below $25,000 to avoid VP review. Which control enhancement would MOST effectively address this circumvention?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Lowering the VP approval threshold from $25,000 to $10,000", "Implementing an automated control that aggregates purchase orders by vendor and department within a rolling period and flags cumulative totals exceeding $25,000", "Requiring all purchase orders to receive VP approval regardless of amount", "Removing the automated workflow and reverting to manual paper-based approvals"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS06.03, automated controls should detect circumvention patterns. Aggregating purchase orders by vendor and department within a rolling time period identifies split-ordering patterns that individually fall below thresholds but collectively represent large unauthorized purchases. Wrong (A): Simply lowering the threshold would increase VP workload without addressing the underlying split-ordering behavior, which would shift to the new threshold. Wrong (C): Requiring VP approval on all orders is impractical and creates bottlenecks without targeting the specific risk. Wrong (D): Reverting to manual approvals eliminates automation benefits and likely worsens control effectiveness.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — ERP upgrade risk from excessive customizations
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Whitfield Industries has heavily customized its ERP revenue recognition module over five years with 47 custom code modifications. The ERP vendor releases a major version upgrade with enhanced ASC 606 compliance features. The IT director estimates the upgrade will require retesting all 47 customizations at a cost of $380,000 and a six-month timeline. Which ERP risk does this situation BEST illustrate, and what should the IT steering committee evaluate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Single point of failure risk; evaluate implementing redundant ERP instances", "Data migration risk; evaluate whether historical data can be converted to the new version format", "Customization risk; evaluate whether custom modifications can be replaced by standard functionality in the new version", "Vendor lock-in risk; evaluate switching to a different ERP platform"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT BAI06.01, excessive ERP customization introduces significant upgrade risk because every custom modification must be retested and potentially rewritten with each vendor release. The steering committee should evaluate whether the new version's standard features can replace custom code, reducing future upgrade costs. Wrong (A): Single point of failure relates to operational availability during outages, not upgrade complexity. Wrong (B): Data migration is a concern during system conversions but is secondary to the code compatibility issue described. Wrong (D): Vendor lock-in is a strategic concern but does not address the immediate problem of managing 47 customizations during an upgrade.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Shared database as ERP characteristic
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$What is the defining characteristic that distinguishes an ERP system from a collection of standalone business applications?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Use of cloud-based hosting", "Requirement for annual software licensing", "A single shared database integrating all business modules", "Availability of mobile device access"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT APO03.02, the defining feature of an ERP system is a single shared database that integrates core business processes so that a transaction in one module simultaneously updates related modules. This eliminates data silos and inconsistencies. Wrong (A): Cloud hosting is a deployment option available to both ERP and standalone applications. Wrong (B): Annual licensing is a commercial model unrelated to system architecture. Wrong (D): Mobile access is a feature that can be added to any application and does not define ERP integration.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Exception reporting for data validation at interfaces
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Oakmont Financial's bank feed interface imports 1,200 transactions daily into the ERP general ledger. The system generates a report listing 15 transactions where the bank's account codes could not be mapped to valid GL accounts. This report is an example of which integration control?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exception reporting for data validation at integration points", "Batch control total reconciliation", "Output distribution control", "Segregation of duties enforcement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT DSS06.04, exception reporting at integration points identifies transactions that fail data validation rules during transfer. Flagging unmapped account codes allows timely correction before mispostings occur. Wrong (B): Batch control total reconciliation compares aggregate counts and amounts, not individual field-level mapping failures. Wrong (C): Output distribution controls govern who receives reports, not the detection of mapping errors. Wrong (D): Segregation of duties relates to role separation, not data validation during system interfaces.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Encryption in transit at integration points and residual risk
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Meridian Healthcare transmits patient billing data between its EHR system and ERP accounts receivable module over an internal network using unencrypted FTP. A security assessment identifies that a network intruder could intercept and modify billing amounts during transmission. The IT team proposes switching to SFTP with TLS encryption. After implementing SFTP, which residual integration risk would still require a SEPARATE mitigating control?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Data interception during transmission", "Data corruption from field-mapping errors between the EHR and ERP", "Unauthorized access to the FTP server", "Man-in-the-middle attacks on the network"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS05.02, SFTP with TLS addresses interception and tampering during transmission, but encryption does not validate whether data fields are correctly mapped between source and target systems. Field-mapping errors require separate data validation rules and exception reporting. Wrong (A): SFTP with TLS encryption directly prevents data interception during transit. Wrong (C): SFTP includes authentication mechanisms that address unauthorized server access. Wrong (D): TLS encryption in SFTP prevents man-in-the-middle attacks by authenticating both endpoints and encrypting the communication channel.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Completeness testing via batch-to-batch reconciliation
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Summit Corp's ERP processes accounts payable invoices in daily batches. The AP supervisor notes that yesterday's batch closing balance was $1,247,000, and today's batch includes $389,000 in new invoices and $215,000 in payments. The expected closing balance should be $1,421,000. The actual closing balance is $1,396,000 — a $25,000 discrepancy. Which batch control technique identified this variance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hash total comparison", "Record count verification", "Run-to-run total reconciliation", "Financial total validation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT DSS06.02, a run-to-run total reconciles the ending balance of one processing cycle to the beginning of the next, adding new inputs and subtracting processed outputs. Comparing the expected closing balance ($1,247,000 + $389,000 - $215,000 = $1,421,000) to the actual ($1,396,000) reveals the $25,000 discrepancy. Wrong (A): Hash totals sum non-financial fields to verify batch completeness, not monetary balances. Wrong (B): Record count verification checks the number of transactions processed but would not detect a dollar amount discrepancy. Wrong (D): Financial total validation sums monetary amounts within a single batch, not across consecutive processing cycles.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — AIS data flow sequence
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Kensington Corp's accounting team traces a customer payment from the bank deposit slip through the AIS to the updated accounts receivable balance and general ledger posting. Which sequence correctly describes the AIS data flow for this transaction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Output → Processing → Storage → Input", "Storage → Input → Output → Processing", "Processing → Output → Input → Storage", "Input → Processing → Output → Storage"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AICPA IT Section guidance, the AIS data flow follows a logical sequence: source documents are captured as input, the system processes them by validating data and applying business rules, processed information is output as reports and updated balances, and data is stored in the database for future retrieval. Wrong (A): This reverses the flow by starting with output. Wrong (B): Storage precedes input in this sequence, which is incorrect — data must be captured before it can be stored. Wrong (C): Processing cannot occur before input is received.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Reorder point in inventory module
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$What is the purpose of a reorder point configured in an ERP inventory module?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To automatically trigger a purchase requisition when stock falls to a predetermined level", "To calculate the weighted average cost of inventory on hand", "To generate financial statements at month-end", "To assign inventory items to specific warehouse locations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT BAI03.03, a reorder point is a predefined inventory threshold that triggers an automated purchase requisition when the quantity on hand falls to that level, preventing stockouts and ensuring continuity of operations. Wrong (B): Weighted average cost calculation is a cost flow method, not a reorder function. Wrong (C): Financial statement generation is a GL function, not an inventory reorder mechanism. Wrong (D): Warehouse location assignment is a logistics function separate from reorder point logic.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — System load differences between batch and real-time
(5, 'ERP and Accounting Information Systems', $EXPL$Hartwell Corp is evaluating whether to process its 3,000 daily inventory transactions in real time or in a nightly batch. The IT manager notes that real-time processing would distribute system load throughout the business day, while batch processing concentrates the load into a two-hour overnight window. Which factor does this comparison MOST directly address?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Data accuracy and completeness", "System capacity and performance planning", "Segregation of duties requirements", "Audit trail comprehensiveness"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT DSS01.01, the distribution of system load is a capacity and performance planning consideration. Real-time processing spreads demand evenly, while batch processing creates peak loads during the processing window, requiring sufficient capacity to handle the concentrated workload. Wrong (A): Both methods can achieve data accuracy and completeness with appropriate controls. Wrong (C): Segregation of duties is enforced through role-based access controls, not processing method selection. Wrong (D): Both methods generate audit trails — real-time through individual transaction logs and batch through batch control totals.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
